# Tournova environment variables.
#
# Two files, both git-ignored, both started from this one:
#
#     cp .env.example .env              # development — points at STAGING
#     cp .env.example .env.production   # loaded by `npm run build` — points at PRODUCTION
#
# The split is deliberate. `npm run dev` runs mode=development and reads `.env`, so a
# developer cannot read or write live tournament data by accident. `npm run build` runs
# mode=production and reads `.env.production`, so `npm run deploy` needs no extra flags.
#
# Vite gives `.env.<mode>` precedence over `.env`, and the real environment precedence over
# both. `scripts/verify-build-target.mjs` resolves them in the same order, so the check
# compares a build against the values that build actually used.
#
# Never commit real credentials.

# --- Required -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The public Supabase connection. These are safe to expose to the browser: the
# publishable (anon) key is protected by Row Level Security, not by secrecy.
#
# The app reads the VITE_ pair in the browser bundle and the plain pair during
# server-side rendering, so both forms are needed. They hold the same values.
#
# A build fails if either pair is missing. See src/integrations/supabase/client.ts.

SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# All four are needed in BOTH `.env` and `.env.production`, because the values differ per
# environment. Vite loads `.env` for every mode and lets `.env.<mode>` override it, so a
# variable set only in `.env` is still present in a production build — carrying its
# development value. That silent carry-over is what the two-file split makes visible.
#
# The rule is not limited to these four: it applies to every `VITE_` variable. Deleting one
# from `.env.production` does not remove it from a production build, it hands that build the
# staging value. If `.env` sets a `VITE_` key, `.env.production` has to set it too.

# --- Optional: project identity -------------------------------------------
#
# The project ref: the id in the Supabase dashboard URL and in the hostname of `SUPABASE_URL`
# (`https://<ref>.supabase.co`). It appears under two names because two readers want it and
# neither reads the other's.
#
#   SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID        Read by `npm run db:link`, which passes it to
#                              `supabase link --project-ref`. The shell expands it and npm
#                              does not read `.env`, so it has to be exported to have any
#                              effect:
#
#                                  set -a && source .env && set +a
#
#   VITE_SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID   Read by `scripts/backup-production.mjs`, which takes it from
#                              `.env` and refuses to dump that project - a dump of staging is
#                              not what that script is for. Leave it unset and the refusal
#                              quietly stops happening.
#
# Neither is read by application code, so the `VITE_` prefix buys nothing here. It is spelled
# that way only because the value has to satisfy the carry-over rule above.

# SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=
# VITE_SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=

# --- Optional: server-side and operator scripts ---------------------------
#
# Server-side only. NOT read by the frontend, and MUST NEVER be given a VITE_
# prefix - any variable with that prefix is compiled into the client bundle and
# would leak full database access to every visitor.
#
# Used by src/integrations/supabase/client.server.ts and by the operator scripts
# in scripts/ that reset or seed tournament data. Not needed for development or
# for the test suites.

# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Optional: script authentication --------------------------------------
#
# Which credential an operator script needs depends on what the script does, not on taste,
# so these are not interchangeable.

# The Supabase Management API token, created at
# https://supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens. This is what most operator scripts use:
# scripts/apply-migrations.mjs, backup-production.mjs, restore-backup.mjs, run-sql.mjs and
# create-staff-login.mjs.
#
# It matters because a database password is not always available: `supabase db push` needs
# one, which is exactly why scripts/apply-migrations.mjs exists as the token-only path.

# SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# An alternative to the service-role key for scripts/reset-tournament-data.mjs and
# scripts/fake-day-one-results.mjs: sign in as a real staff account rather than bypassing
# Row Level Security.

# SUPABASE_DESK_EMAIL=
# SUPABASE_DESK_PASSWORD=

# --- Optional: test harness ----------------------------------------------
#
# Credentials for scripts/record-staff-session.mjs, which records a real staff
# session into e2e/.auth/staff.json for manual checks against a live backend.
#
# This is NOT required for `npm run test:e2e`: the Playwright suite is hermetic
# and supplies a synthetic session, so it needs no credentials at all.

# E2E_STAFF_EMAIL=
# E2E_STAFF_PASSWORD=

# Port and base URL for the Playwright web server. Defaults to 8080.
# PLAYWRIGHT_PORT=
# PLAYWRIGHT_BASE_URL=

# --- Optional: deploying the Worker ---------------------------------------
#
# Cloudflare API token for wrangler, used by `npm run deploy` and `npm run deploy:check`.
# Create one at https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens, with Workers Scripts: Edit on
# the account that owns the `tournova-ops` Worker.
#
# It belongs in `.env` only: it is account-level rather than per-environment, and wrangler
# loads `.env` itself, so no export is needed.
#
# Without it the build still succeeds, and the failure lands later and less clearly, as
# wrangler reporting "You are not authenticated".

# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# --- Optional: backups and recovery --------------------------------------
#
# The database password for the production project, from the dashboard under
# Project Settings -> Database. `npm run backup:production` and `npm run restore:to-local`
# authenticate as the database role, so a publishable key cannot stand in for it.
#
# Read from the environment first, then `.env.local`, then `.env`, so an explicit export
# always wins. `.env.local` is the better home for it if you would rather keep it apart from
# the connection values; that file is git-ignored too.
#
# Read docs/recovery-runbook.md before relying on a dump.

# SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD=
